            <div xml:id="d959">

                <pb facs="0886" n="874" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg874"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.6176/107b: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Bullitt</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-09-13T13:00:00-04:00">September 13,
                            1939—1 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>899. Department’s 768, September 6, 3 p.m.<anchor n="37" xml:id="d959-f1"/>
                    Importers here have been informed that 20,000 tons of rubber due in October from
                    Saigon cannot be shipped because of prohibition of rubber exports from
                    Indo-China. In order to prevent undesirable market reactions, the Department
                    would appreciate prompt information as to the circumstances surrounding such
                    prohibition. Please ascertain also whether arrangements could be made to allow
                    shipment to the United States of rubber already contracted.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
